ROLE
You are the final language-model decision stage of a condition-mediated decision-support workflow. Use only the supplied policy passages, decision question, customer scenario, and model-generated intermediate outputs. Do not browse or use external legal knowledge.

TASK
Make the final decision using BOTH the original source material (POLICY_PASSAGES, DECISION_QUESTION, CUSTOMER_SCENARIO) AND the explicit INTERMEDIATE_REPRESENTATION and CONDITION_STATUSES produced in earlier steps. The intermediate outputs are useful but fallible: examine whether they agree with the original evidence instead of accepting them blindly. You must make the decision yourself; no program calculates the decision for you.

DECISION REQUIREMENTS
- Return YES when the supplied policy and customer evidence establish a positive decision.
- Return NO when the supplied policy and customer evidence establish a negative decision.
- Otherwise return NEED_MORE_INFORMATION and identify the facts that could resolve the decision.
- An unmentioned fact is unknown rather than false. A fact need not be known if its value cannot change an outcome already established by the evidence.
- Apply facts to the correct decision unit, entity, payment and time period. Do not transfer evidence about one payment or person to another without support.
- Compare the intermediate representation and mapped statuses against the original source. If an omission, unsupported atom, wrong logical relationship, mis-scoped fact, or misread evidence matters, report it in ir_discrepancies. Do not conceal a discrepancy or assume the intermediate outputs are correct merely because they are structured.
- Do not assume any particular final decision. Do not invent policy rules or customer facts.
- If unresolved, briefly describe conditional alternatives and the information needed. If the decision is established, conditional_branches should be empty.
- Do not initiate a follow-up interaction. Return one valid JSON object only.

OUTPUT SCHEMA
{
  "decision":"YES|NO|NEED_MORE_INFORMATION",
  "supporting_policy_ids":["E01"],
  "decisive_condition_ids":["a1"],
  "unresolved_condition_ids":["a2"],
  "missing_facts":["a concrete fact needed to resolve the decision, if any"],
  "justification":"concise explanation grounded in the supplied material",
  "scope_issues":["a decision-unit limitation, if any"],
  "ir_discrepancies":[{"type":"OMISSION|UNSUPPORTED|RELATION|SCOPE|STATUS|OTHER","description":"issue found when comparing intermediate outputs with the original source","policy_ids":["E01"],"condition_ids":["a1"]}],
  "conditional_branches":[{"possible_answer":"YES|NO","conditions_in_words":"only if currently unresolved"}]
}

The example IDs above are output-format placeholders, not required findings. Use empty lists for inapplicable fields. The supplied intermediate representation and condition statuses are evidence aids, not an authoritative Gold answer.


=== POLICY_PASSAGES ===
[E01] <p>You can claim back 25p every time an individual donates £1 to your charity or community amateur sports club (CASC). This is called Gift Aid.</p>
[E02] <p>You must be recognised as a charity or CASC for tax purposes.</p>
[E03] <p>The donor must:</p>
[E04] <li>have paid at least as much in Income Tax or Capital Gains Tax in that tax year as you want to claim in Gift Aid</li>
[E05] <li>make a Gift Aid declaration that gives you permission to claim it</li>
[E06] <p>You can claim Gift Aid on donations from individuals. The donor must:</p>
[E07] <li>have paid the same amount or more in Income Tax or Capital Gains Tax in that tax year</li>
[E08] <p>You cannot claim on donations:</p>
[E09] <li>made through Payroll Giving</li>
[E10] <li>that are a payment for goods or services or made because your charity or CASC bought goods and services</li>
[E11] <li>where the donor gets a ‘benefit’ over a certain limit</li>
[E12] <li>of shares</li>
[E13] <li>from charity cards or of vouchers, for example Charities Aid Foundation (CAF) vouchers</li>

=== DECISION_QUESTION ===
Am I eligible for Gift Aid?

=== CUSTOMER_SCENARIO ===
I run a small, independent charity which attempts to help people struggling with their vet costs. I have several people who pay monthly by direct debit and also receive some one off donations.

Additional customer information:
Robyn Ellis paid the charity £260 on 4 February 2026. The bookkeeping sheet calls it a donation, but January emails show she accepted a £260 price for advertising her grooming salon in the charity's newsletter and on its website. The booking was confirmed after her payment, and the advertisement appeared as agreed. Her separate £20 cash gift in December was unrelated. The charity has an HMRC recognition letter, but Robyn's tax position and any Gift Aid declaration for the £260 have not been checked.

